WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.748
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.920
MINKO GECHEV: Hello, everyone.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.080
My name is Minko Gechev.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.240
I lead developer relations
for Angular at Google.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.600
Today I will share with you
how to host a Modern Angular

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.570
application with
Google Cloud platform.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.180
We can render a single-page
application implemented

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.880
in Angular or any other
frontend framework

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.800
in three different ways.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.550
Client-side rendering, where the
browser receives an index HTML

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.380
file with reference scripts.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.040
Once the scripts load, they
render the application.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.920
Prerendering, or
static site generation,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.390
is where at build time, we
render an HTML file per route.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:43.990
While the browser loads
the HTML file for a route,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:48.280
it renders it and loads
the reference JavaScript.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.710
The page becomes
interactive once the browser

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.350
executes all the JavaScript.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.190
Finally, there is Server-Side
Rendering, or SSR.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.530
SSR is very similar
to prerendering,

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.480
with the main difference
being that rendering

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.330
happens at request time
by a rendering server.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.020
Something in common between
static site generation,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.050
or prerendering, and
client-side rendering,

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:17.140
is that in both cases, we only
deal with static resources.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.040
On the other side, in
SSR, we need a server

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.580
to render the application and
produce the application's HTML

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.830
at request time.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.140
To demonstrate the different
rendering strategies

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.740
and approaches and explore
optimal deployment,

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.190
we will use the Kitty
Cinema application.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.670
Kitty Cinema is a
catalog of cat movies.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.360
We can explore a list of movies
with their names, genres,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.770
and ratings.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.710
When we select a movie,
we can get further details

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.680
about the synopsis,
the cast, as well

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.600
to explore similar
recommended movies.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.200
For this application, we can
use client-side rendering.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.150
The deployment could
be pretty simple.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:05.040
First, we build the application
and get its production assets.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.980
After that, we can upload
the assets using the Firebase

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.270
tooling.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.140
Finally, the application
will land on Firebase hosting

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.980
and be globally
available via our CDN.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.350
Currently, we can achieve
this in a few different ways.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:24.570
First, we can use the Firebase
Node.js command line tools.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.570
We can install them
by using npm globally,

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.320
running npm i -g firebase.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.800
After that, we can go to the
Angular CLI Workspace directory

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.920
and initialize a
Firebase project.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.170
We can run Firebase in that.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.860
After that choose Configure
for Firebase Hosting.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.000
Select a project
you want to deploy,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.400
specify a public directory--

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.660
in this case, the Angular
CLI uses the output directory

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.933
dist/movies/browser.

00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:55.350
After that, we're
going to rewrite

00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:01.020
all the URLs to index.html to
make sure that Angular provides

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.730
client-side routing.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.520
We're going to disable the
deployment with GitHub.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.950
We're not going to overwrite
index.html at this point.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.610
Since we have already built the
application, as the next step,

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.880
just type firebase deploy
and allow the tooling

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.680
to deploy the app.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.500
Once all the files are
in Firebase hosting,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.690
click on the output
URL to preview the app.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.350
An alternative approach
is using Angular CLI.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:31.760
You can run ng add angular/fire
to add Firebase integration

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.110
to your project.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.650
Select that you'd like
to set up ng deploy.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.860
Select an account you will
want to use, and next,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.320
select or create a new project.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.410
After that, select
or create a new site.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.600
And finally, just run ng deploy.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.660
This command will build
your project and upload

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.240
the static assets
to Firebase hosting.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.870
You can preview the result
following the output URL.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.610
Let us track step by step
the sequence of events when

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.530
the user opens the application.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.160
Once they enter the
URL, their browser

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.410
will send a request
to Firebase hosting

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.740
and download the
index.html that is

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.540
the page for the application.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:18.620
The browser will render the
HTML and show a progress bar.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.860
Next, the browser will download
all the reference JavaScript

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.990
within index.html.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.320
Next, the browser will
execute the JavaScript.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.300
Notice that at this
point, the user

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.670
still sees only a progress bar.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.740
Until the rendering
completes, the page

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.570
will not be too useful.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.820
At this step, Angular
will take over

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.280
and render the user
interface, making the page

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.200
useful and interactive.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.560
For three out of the
four steps, the user

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.570
only sees a progress bar.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:50.260
That's not the best
user experience.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.060
Depending on the network
connection speed,

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.000
it may take tens of
seconds for the application

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.150
to show useful information.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.400
In this particular
case, we don't

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.730
need to make the application
interactive immediately.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.950
This is a content
website, which means

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.740
that even if you only
show the movie list,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.170
the page will be still useful.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.420
After that, we can load
the associated JavaScript

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.190
and add interactivity.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.470
Let's explore a more
optimal deployment

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.810
strategy for the Kitty
Cinema application.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.360
This way, we're going
to quickly serve

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.580
a useful page for the user and
later on make it interactive.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.500
We can select a
suitable strategy

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.760
by identifying the different
types of content we can render

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.690
and taking performance
into consideration.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.150
Ideally, we would want
to prerender everything

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:45.110
at build time so that we can
deploy the resources to a CDN

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.410
and deliver them from the
closest location to the user

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.030
possible.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:54.800
Often, pages depend on dynamic
datastore or user session

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:55.850
information.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.330
When we need to store
personalized content on a page,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:02.600
it becomes infeasible to
generate it at build time.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:08.080
We're going to consider
two rendering strategies.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.600
First one is
prerendering, for pages

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.640
that don't change too often.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.600
Serving them from a CDN will
be cheap and really fast.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.330
We don't have to do any
work at request time,

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.790
and we can deliver
them from the closest

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.640
to the user geographical
location available.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.630
For pages that need to
show personalized content

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.930
or depend on a very frequently
changing data source,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.040
we can use
server-side rendering.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.340
It's usually more technically
complicated and expensive

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.130
to serve such pages,
because at request time

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:45.030
we have to run the application
server, render the app,

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:48.480
and deliver the produced
HTML to the user.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.340
For prerendered content, we
can use Firebase Hosting.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.770
This is content
delivery network,

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:58.490
allows us to serve the
prerendered pages from a close

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.440
to the user location.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.010
For server-side rendering,
we'll use Cloud Functions.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.590
They provide an on-demand
execution environment

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.460
which allows us to handle
individual requests,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.340
render our single page
application in Node.js,

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.410
and serve it to the client.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.740
To prerender and server-side
render our application,

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.860
we're going to use
Angular Universal.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.180
Just run in the terminal ng add
@nguniversal/express-engine,

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.630
and Angular CLI will set
everything up automatically

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.740
for you.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.650
Earlier this year,
Google Cloud announced

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.500
Cloud Functions
Second Generation,

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.230
powered by Cloud Run.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.350
This new version provides
more powerful infrastructure

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.250
and advanced control on
scalability and performance.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.520
They offer longer
processing times and larger

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.890
upgraded instances, with
more memory and virtual CPUs.

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:59.310
To deploy our application,
we'll use Firebase SDK

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.820
for Cloud Functions, which
allows us to configure them

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.830
through code.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.410
The Firebase SDK
allows us to only run

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:11.370
Node.js in the function, which
fits our goals really well.

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.800
In case you want to
use other technologies,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.570
check out this
quick starting guide

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:19.540
for Cloud Functions in
Google Cloud Console.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:22.150
Let us have a look at two
different pages in the app

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.220
and identify the
optimal rendering

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.640
strategy for each one of them.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.280
We have a generic page that
shows the popular kitty movies.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.190
We also have a page that
shows user's favorites.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.700
The generic page could
be a good candidate

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.630
for prerendering if the popular
movies don't change too often.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.760
If they do change
often, though, we'll

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:46.770
have to regenerate
the application

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.080
and redeploy it very frequently.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.180
In such a case, we
might be better off

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.930
using server-side
rendering for it.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.820
The page showing the
user's favorite movies

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.770
depends on the session ID, which
means that we can't generate it

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:05.040
at build time, and we would have
to use server-side rendering.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.190
An approach where we use
server-side rendering

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:12.180
for some of the pages in the
app and prerendering for others

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.920
is called hybrid rendering.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.670
With hybrid rendering, we can
deploy the static resources

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:22.030
of the application-- for
example, JavaScript images,

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.870
prerendered pages, et cetera--

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:26.980
to a CDN, for example
with Firebase Hosting.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.200
The pages containing
the dynamic content

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.000
will render in Cloud Functions
with server-side rendering.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.820
Let us look into how to deploy
an application to Firebase

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:40.160
Hosting that uses
hybrid rendering.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.940
To set up hybrid rendering,
we can make a few adjustments

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.010
to firebase.json.

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:49.520
In this file, we can
specify a predeploy command

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.160
to our function and its source.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.520
Here we can specify that we want
to build the Angular Universal

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.380
server before we
deploy the function.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.250
Next we can specify the
hosting configuration.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:05.120
We will set the public directory
to dist/movies/browser.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.920
We will use cleanUrls to
strip the .html suffixes,

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.260
and also set
trailingSlash to false.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.250
Notice that we also
add the star star

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:19.130
rewrites to the SSR function.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.470
For optimal results, make
sure that you also review

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.500
the best practices for caching.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.050
Now let us compare the
original client-side rendering

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:29.630
application with the
latest deployment that

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:32.440
uses hybrid rendering.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.190
On the left side, you can
see the client-side rendered

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:38.270
application, and
on the right side,

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.970
you can see the
prerendered application.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:44.690
Both are delivered from a CDN,
but the client-side rendered

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:47.210
application needs
to load JavaScript

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:50.670
before it can show any
contents to the user.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.570
Notice how the prerendered
app loads and rendered

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:57.020
in half of the time needed
for the client-site rendered

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.000
alternative.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.920
Selecting the correct
rendering strategy

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:05.750
can have a significant impact on
your application's performance.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.790
All right, that's most of
what I had for you today.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.270
Before we end, however,
I want to share one more

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:13.460
thing you can do
to further automate

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:17.450
the process of deployment
of hybrid applications using

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.196
Incremental Static Generation.

00:11:20.196 --> 00:11:22.710
We already mentioned
in what cases we should

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.870
use prerendering
and when we should

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:26.880
go with server-side rendering.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.960
In short, if a page is
user-specific or changes

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.510
very frequently,
we most certainly

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:36.510
want to use server-side
rendering for it.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:41.690
We also discussed that we
prerender pages at build time.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:46.480
Let us look into incremental
approach to prerendering.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.110
To achieve incremental
prerendering, or static site

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:54.790
generation, we can use
automation via the APIs

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.170
that Firestore offers.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.770
Let's suppose that we
store all the content

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:03.440
that our static pages
depend on in Firestore.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.850
When we update the
Firestore data,

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.820
we can get a
Firestore change event

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:13.040
that triggers the Incremental
Static Regeneration.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.950
Once we regenerate
pages that changed,

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:19.070
we can just deploy them
to Firebase Hosting.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:22.130
Deploying to Firebase Hosting
purges the cache content

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.880
from the CDN, so we
will want to balance

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:29.300
deploying on every change
versus regenerating

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:32.690
static content periodically,
while still falling back

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.220
to SSR.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.260
That's everything I
had for you today.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.150
In this presentation,
we had a look

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.130
into different
prerendering strategies

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.550
for single-page applications
and most optimal deployment

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:46.860
techniques for each one of them.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.620
For more content, make sure
you follow Angular on Twitter

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.530
and subscribe to the
Angular YouTube channel.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.030
See you next time,
and happy coding.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

